WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.629
Popularized by films like Alfred Hitchcock's
1948 thriller Rope, and made famous by recent

00:00:05.629 --> 00:00:10.480
hits like 2014's Oscar winner Birdman, long
takes where the director follows the narrative

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.919
with a single shot for the length of an entire
scene or longer can now be found everywhere.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:18.180
Some are nearly invisible unless you know
what you're looking for, while others are

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.250
masterful tour de forces meant to grab the
audience's attention.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:25.440
Here are some of our favorite long takes from
film and television, along with our take on

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.449
what makes them so special.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:29.210
Raiders of the Lost Ark

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.489
It's only 96 seconds, but the scene introducing
the character of Marion Ravenwood in Stephen

00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:38.079
Spielberg's 1981 adventure film Raiders of
the Lost Ark is a classic example of what's

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.370
come to be known as a "Spielberg Oner."

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:44.770
With a few notable exceptions, Spielberg Oners
tend to be unobtrusive — you don't realize

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:47.270
you're even looking at a long take in many
cases.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.430
The one from Raiders is particularly noteworthy
because it does such a great job of introducing

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:55.539
the audience to Marion, while also adding
some tension to the drinking contest and heightening

00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:59.010
the hilarity of her opponent's eventual collapse.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:00.530
True Detective

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.900
While well-executed long take tracking shots
have long been featured in movies, in recent

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.860
years, they've started to pop up in television
productions more frequently.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:13.380
In 2014, HBO set the bar during season one,
episode four of their crime anthology drama

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.860
True Detective, starring Matthew McConaughey
and Woody Harrelson.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.890
Directed by Cary Joji Fukunaga, the six-minute
long take at the end of the episode follows

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:25.470
McConaughey through a complex scene involving
a gang robbery gone wrong, several shootouts,

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.540
a hostage-taking and chase through multiple
houses, and an escape over a fence into a

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:30.690
waiting vehicle.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:34.720
It's an amazing take in a great episode, one
that propelled True Detective into the mainstream,

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.110
earning Fukunaga and the rest of the crew
multiple Primetime Emmy Awards wins and nominations.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.500
Game of Thrones

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:45.670
In "Battle of the Bastards," the ninth episode
of season six on the hit HBO series Game of

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.170
Thrones, director Miguel Sapochnik pulled
off a long take tracking shot following protagonist

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:54.390
Jon Snow as he navigates through a pitched
battle between his army and Ramsay Bolton's

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:55.390
forces.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:59.540
Clocking in at 60 seconds, it gives audiences
a visceral and bloody look inside a frenetic

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.710
battle, making it perhaps one of the most
realistic depictions of a medieval clash between

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:04.710
armies.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.280
Audiences and critics alike loved the episode;
it remains one of the highest-rated by viewers

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.980
on IMDb, and it won a record-setting seven
Primetime Emmy Awards.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:13.980
Oldboy

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.770
Not only is the "hallway fight" scene from
Park Chan-wook's 2003 noir thriller Oldboy

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:23.170
one of the best action sequences to be found
anywhere, it's also an impressive three-minute

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.170
long take.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:28.730
The cinematography isn't anything complex;
the motion of the camera simply slides sideways,

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.390
following star Choi Min-sik as he advances
down a hallway while beating up a group of

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:33.390
thugs.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:36.530
However, the simplicity works, especially
when paired with the gritty surroundings and

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.030
simple fighting techniques.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:41.680
The scene's reminiscent of old beat-'em-up
arcade games like Double Dragon — if Double

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.120
Dragon allowed you to take on dozens of bad
guys at once and bash their heads in with

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.910
a claw hammer.

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:47.970
Gravity

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.490
Already known for his groundbreaking long
take car scene from 2006's Children of Men,

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:57.220
Alfonso Cuarón one-upped himself with the
release of the 2013 science fiction thriller

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.220
Gravity.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.650
The film opens with an amazing 17-minute long
take, beginning with a stunning view of Earth

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.260
from orbit and ending with Sandra Bullock's
astronaut flung away into the blackness of

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.260
outer space.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.960
While Cuarón won't give away all the secrets
behind the making of this shot, we do know

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:15.290
the crew created a special cage-like lighting
rig, which was able to rotate lights around

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:19.400
the actors at high speed to give the illusion
they were spinning or flipping in zero gravity.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.069
No matter how it was achieved, there's no
question that this long take will remain Cuarón's

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:27.170
calling card for a long while — until he
raises the bar yet again.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.340
The Secret in their Eyes

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:34.150
Directed, co-written, produced, and edited
by Argentinian filmmaker Juan José Campanella,

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:38.870
the 2009 crime drama The Secret in their Eyes
won an Oscar for Best Foreign Language Film.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:39.870
And for good reason.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.090
The film features several long takes, including
a visually stunning five-minute shot that

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.470
begins with an aerial view of a packed soccer
stadium before closing in on the main characters

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:52.720
in the crowd and following them Steadicam-style
as they pursue a suspect through its underbelly

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.760
and finally out on the field itself.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:55.860
Awesome.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.440
Weekend

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:02.870
In his 1967 black comedy Weekend, French director
Jean-Luc Godard presented the audience with

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:07.530
a thoroughly unlikeable couple — so uncaring
that they each plan to murder the other in

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.430
order to escape the relationship.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.209
Before they can do that, however, they need
to take a cross-country trip to secure a pending

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:14.209
inheritance.

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:17.989
As the embittered pair embark on their journey,
however , they — and the film itself — are

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:21.250
interrupted by a massive traffic jam along
a country road.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:25.440
Orchestrated masterfully by Godard, the crowd
of cars seems to stretch for almost a mile

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.130
and takes the bickering couple nearly eight
minutes of screen time to navigate, as they

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.440
illegally pass and essentially road-rage their
way through.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.470
When we finally reach the cause of the holdup
— a horrifyingly bloody car accident — the

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:40.590
ruthlessness of the couple becomes evident
as they and the camera speed past without

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:44.440
a pause, relieved to finally get on with the
rest of the movie at any cost.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:45.440
Thanks for watching!

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:47.419
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.449
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

